   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "It is done to you",
     "texto": [
      "When another person does something to your body or to your things, the pronoun **me, te, le, nos, os, les** says who receives it.\n\n- **Me han cortado** el pelo. (Someone has cut my hair.)\n- **Le han robado** la cartera.\n- **Te han arreglado** el grifo.\n\nThe verb is in the **ellos** form (han cortado, robaron, arreglaron) because it does not matter who did it. **Me he cortado el pelo** may mean you did it yourself; with **me han cortado**, someone else did."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Pronoun + article, not «mi» or «su»",
     "texto": [
      "With the body, clothes and personal things, Spanish uses the **pronoun** and the **article**:\n\n- **Me** han manchado **la** camisa. (not «mi camisa»)\n- **Le** han robado **la** cartera.\n- **Te** han sacado **una** muela.\n\nThe verb is in the third person plural (**han manchado**, **robaron**, **arreglaron**) when you do not know or do not care who did it.\n\nTo offer something to someone you use the same pronoun: ¿**Te hago** un café? ¿**Os llevo** las maletas?"
     ],
